Adjudicated 2026-09-17. Four arms, two PASS; both ifm routes refused on their daily token cap and
claude-sonnet is disabled.

  codex-luna    +265 lines  47 tests
  glm-53-flash  +356 lines  53 tests

Both scored 11 of 11, with byte-identical output on the real figures:

    Differs { ours_usd: 16.8747, theirs_usd: 20.0299,
              residual_usd: 3.1552, fraction: 0.15752450087119757 }

Both chose the provider's total as the denominator without being told which to use, and both got
the SIGN right -- a positive residual means we under-counted, which is the only conclusion the
function exists to support and the one that inverts if it is backwards.

WHAT SEPARATED THEM was the boundary I deliberately left open: a negative tolerance_fraction.

    codex-luna     Agrees
    glm-53-flash   Unchecked

Both "said what they do", which is all the spec demanded. But a negative tolerance is a caller
bug, and returning Agrees from it is a confident answer derived from a nonsensical configuration
-- the exact shape this project exists to refuse. glm declines to decide. That is the right
reading of its own Unchecked doc: "One side is unavailable, so no comparison was made. NOT
agreement." It stretches the variant slightly, since the missing thing is a valid tolerance
rather than a figure, and it is still the better answer.

Everything else agreed, including the cases most likely to diverge: exactly at tolerance is
Agrees for both, with a hair either side landing correctly; NaN and infinity are Unchecked for
both, never Agrees; and theirs = 0 with ours = 5 yields a finite fraction rather than an
infinity.

53 tests to 47, and the free route. 1405 tests, clippy 9.

NOTE ON THE GAP. The machine sat idle through a long stretch of ticks with the queue empty and
the autopilot alive but with nothing to launch -- live=0 waves=0 queued=0. The autopilot only
